The following patterns constitute automatic failure of Tier 1 certification. Any skill containing these patterns cannot receive a scanned, verified, or certified label regardless of context or justification. 2.2.1 Code Execution Primitives​ Pattern Regex Rationale eval()  eval\s*\( Arbitrary code execution; no legitimate use in a skill exec()  exec\s*\( Shell command execution via code Function() constructor  new\s+Function\s*\( Runtime code generation; equivalent to eval child_process  child_process  Direct subprocess spawning outside agent tool system Invoke-Expression  Invoke-Expression  PowerShell equivalent of eval 2.2.2 Remote Code Execution​ Pattern Regex Rationale curl | bash curl\s+[^
|]*|\s*(ba)?sh Downloads and executes arbitrary remote code wget | bash wget\s+[^
|]*|\s*(ba)?sh Same as above via wget Piped shell execution |\s*(ba)?sh  Generic piped execution pattern 2.2.3 Obfuscation Techniques​ Pattern Regex Rationale Base64 decode (atob)  atob\s*\( Used to hide malicious payloads from scanners Base64 decode (btoa)  btoa\s*\( Encoding data for exfiltration Base64 CLI decode  base64\s+(-[dD]|--decode)  Command-line base64 decoding (Linux -d and macOS -D) Hex-encoded content \x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}(?:\x[0-9a-fA-F]{2}){3,} Multi-byte hex sequences used for payload hiding Password-protected archives  unzip\s+-P | 7z\s+x\s+-p Extracting archives with embedded passwords to evade scanning 2.2.4 Credential Access​ Pattern Regex Rationale SSH directory ~/\.ssh/|\$HOME/\.ssh/ SSH key theft AWS credentials ~/\.aws/|\$HOME/\.aws/ Cloud credential theft Wallet paths ~/\.ethereum/|~/\.bitcoin/|\.wallet Cryptocurrency wallet theft .env file reading  cat\s+[^
]*\.env |readFile[^
]*\.env Environment variable/secret extraction credentials.json  credentials\.json  GCP/service account credential access secrets.yaml  secrets\.yaml  Kubernetes secret access 2.2.5 Memory Poisoning​ Pattern Regex Rationale SOUL.md writes write.*SOUL\.md|edit.*SOUL\.md|create.*SOUL\.md Persistent prompt injection via agent memory MEMORY.md writes write.*MEMORY\.md|edit.*MEMORY\.md|create.*MEMORY\.md Persistent prompt injection via agent memory CLAUDE.md writes write.*CLAUDE\.md|edit.*CLAUDE\.md|create.*CLAUDE\.md Agent configuration tampering Agent config writes write.*\.claude/|edit.*\.claude/ Modifying agent settings to persist malicious behavior 2.2.6 Data Exfiltration​ Pattern Regex Rationale curl --data / curl -d curl\s+.*(-d |--data ) Uploading data to external endpoints for exfiltration 2.2.7 Destructive Commands​ Pattern Regex Rationale rm -rf  rm\s+-[a-z]*r[a-z]*f| rm\s+-rf  Recursive force deletion format drive  format\s+[a-zA-Z]: Disk formatting DROP TABLE/DATABASE  DROP\s+(TABLE|DATABASE)  Database destruction dd disk wipe  dd\s+if=.* of=/dev/ Low-level disk overwrite mkfs  mkfs  Filesystem creation (erases existing data) chmod 777  chmod\s+(-R\s+)?777  World-writable permissions Safe context exceptions Some patterns have legitimate uses. For example, rm -rf in a temporary directory ($TMPDIR, /tmp/) is standard cleanup. The Tier 1 scanner applies safe-context exceptions where the surrounding code demonstrates the operation targets a safe path. However, forbidden patterns in Section 2.2 have NO safe-context exceptions — they fail unconditionally.
